{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1496498-new-st-question-spirit-confusion","id":1496498,"title":"New ST Question - Spirit Confusion","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-11-08T08:33:26-08:00","id":1496498,"title":"New ST Question - Spirit Confusion","content":"Hello! I am in the process of creating my version of Chicago for an upcoming game of W20 and just started putting down the initial outline of what I want the starting Septs/Packs to look like and the concept of Spirits and Penumbra is sort of confusing me.<br /> <br /> 1. The Sept that the Chronicle will be centered on is surrounding a newly formed level 1 Caern. The players will be forming one of 3 packs that are tasked with protecting the Caern. The Sept is an off shoot of the primary Sept of Chicago, both of which will be dominated by Glass Walkers. The newly formed Sept will be a music recording company, and the Caern's totem spirit will be some sort of Spirit of Music. I really can't find anything suitable in the books that I have. Is a Sept Spirit always an Incarna? Are there Incarna Music Spirits? Any suggestions?<br /> <br /> 2. The PC pack totem is the City Father of Chicago. Is a Pack totem always an Icarna? Jaggling?<br /> <br /> 3. Do the pack totems follow the pack around in the Penumbra? If not, where do they hang out? Does the Sept totem hang out at the sept?<br /> <br /> 4. Whats stopping a bunch of weaver/bane spirits from attacking a Caern in the Penumbra? Is there a bunch of guardian spirits? Do the werewolves have to sit in the penumbra 24/7 watching?<br /> <br /> 5. Ive read the corebook w20 along the umbra book w20. Any other books you'd suggest for spirit stuff?<br /> <br /> Thanks!!<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27612-awesomeotter","name":"Awesomeotter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27612_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-11-08T09:24:54-08:00","id":1496502,"title":"","content":"Note that it's usually very hard to make new Caerns. The act of making one drags every Wyrmish monster in hundred of kilometers around it to pop in and say hi and interrupt the consecration of the Caern. This can be especially hard in the middle of a City, but Glass Walkers are relatively good at it.It might be better to say this was a recovered Caern rather than a newly made one. What's under the Studio? Is it possible something was buried beneath during the golden age of Chicago?<br /> <br /> The Sept is an area around a single Caern serving its needs and protecting it. So you might have a couple of Septs in and around Chicago. Some of them will be bigger and better staffed, and as such they will have more influence than others. It's also possible that if this Caern is new, the people in it have come from one of the other Septs and have positive and/or negative relationships with this one.<br /> <br /> You seem to have a bit of a strange grasp of how spirits work, so I'll preempt your questions with a bit of context. Celstines are the greatest of Spirits and they usually embody the greatest of concepts like say the Planets, Sun and Moon. Incarna are powerful spirits that have broad purview and great power and some of them serve as Totems to the Garou and other Shifters. They also have servants, independant representations of their purview - Jagglings, and lesser representations of their purviews - gafflings. These represent their &quot;Broods&quot; Jagglings are experienced and capable servitors of the Incarna capable of independant action in service to their concept. Gafflings are minor spirits barely able to be anything but what they represent. Usually the Totems of packs are high powered gafflings or low to mid powered Jagglings in service to an Incarna. The totems of Caerns are usually low to high powered Jagglings.<br /> <br /> <br /> 1.) Anything can be a Caern spirit but it's tied to the essence of the place that the Caern is. This is a unique spirit with its own idenitity, ideals and needs. It belongs to a certain brood or class of spirits and has relationships with other local and global spirits. Incarna are too great spirits to be bound to a place like this - if they were this would undoubtedly be a legendary Rank 6 Caern, not a small Rank 1. Now, a spirit of music is a bit strange, because spirits of mental concepts usually belong to the Astral realm and have their own alien logic based on being the concept. That makes them harder to understand than most other spirits and weirder to interact with. If you want to stick with the idea of this spirit being the Totem, I'd play up it's individuality and connection to a past that gives it life - maybe it's tied to a particular song or a particular performer? Maybe it even looks like a performer when you interact with it? This spirit will also be a bit of an outlier without a big support network, so he will have all the incentives to make sure his place of power is protected and he has good relationships with the Garou.<br /> <br /> For instance you could have a minor Caern that's a gym in which the spirit is the embodiment of &quot;Eyes of the Tiger&quot; and looked like Rocky's trainer but in a tiger striped jacket or Tony the Tiger.<br /> <br /> The music spirits themselves would form courts of their own, but those courts usually don't interact much with the real world as they are more tied into the Astral. For instance you could have the Incarna of Symphonies, whose greatest jaggling could be the 5th.<br /> <br /> <br /> 2.) See preamble. By default the City Fathers are high powered Jagglings serving themselves, but one could argue that they all belong the the Brood of the Spirit known as The City (One could see New York, Paris or Tokyo being lower powered Incarna themselves). There are two ways to treat this for the PC pack - by default this would be a lower powered gaffling/Jaggling in service to the City Father, but you could also say that the spirit itself is the one that's their totem - just remember to play up the fact that he's not always available and may be the patron of others.<br /> <br /> In one of our games the City Father of Miami really loved shades, pastel suits, excessive gold jewlery and sports cars. He was the Totem of the local Glass Walker Caern and the Totem of the pack in charge of that Caern.<br /> <br /> 3.) This depends on how you treat 2 and how much the players spend on the totem being available to them. If they pay more for a spirit to be with them more often, then just say it's not the City Father itself but one of his jagglings instead. I refer to steer people into not taking the &quot;always available&quot; perk for totems as it allows for the spirit to get into more trouble.<br /> <br /> If the Spirit is not with them it's usually following the preferences of its purview and being in places symbollic of their power. The City Father might be looking at the city from the top of the oldest highest building in the skyline, racing down Michigan avenue or relaxing in Lincoln Park.<br /> <br /> 4.) By default nothing. That's why there is usually a whole gaggle of spirits in the bawn related to the sept and who help the Garou protect their places. Some of them just serve as warning signs, but others can fight themselves. The more powerful the Caern the more incentive the other spirits have to hang around near it as a place of power and then act in its interests. It's also possible to hide a Caern by keeping a low profile and making sure the local spirits help hide it, this could especially be an option for a recently recovered Caern!<br /> <br /> <br /> 5.) <b>Book of the City</b> (GREAT for any Urban chronicle!), Glass Walkers and Bone Gnawers Tribebooks, Umbra Revised, Guardians of the Caerns, Book of the Weaver, Book of the Wyrm 2nd/20th , Possessed<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-11-08T11:44:26-08:00","id":1496519,"title":"","content":"Here are my responses.<br /> <br /> 1) Caern totems are Jagglings, but these spirits are generally &quot;avatar&quot; spirits that are an aspect of the Incarna. The actual Incarna spirits themselves are very distant spirits that rarely interact directly with Garou. Caern totems arrive sometime after the Rite of Caern Building is performed with the spirit being of a kind that is naturally attuned for the caern location and type. Caern totems are more or less bonded to the caern location and cannot (or won't) leave the caern bawn.<br /> <br /> There is no reason why you can't have Music spirits, but I personally don't remember any such spirits being described. If I had to design one I'd make it low in Rage but high in Gnosis with medium Willpower, and then pick or invent one or more Charms that fit its purpose. I think it is very debatable whether a Music spirit would be more oriented towards Gaia, the Wyld, or Weaver. It probably depends on the specific kind of spirit you want. If the spirit is something like a Woodwind spirit of the wind blowing through reeds, it'd probably be Gaian. If it is more like an inspirational spirit filling a musician with ideas, I'd lean more towards the Wyld. But a spirit representing a fairly advanced kind of music like a symphony or modern instruments, it could be more like a Weaver spirit. Go with your imagination.<br /> <br /> 2) Pack totems are jaggling avatars. So a pack totem of a City Father is not the Incarna of the City Father itself, but a jaggling it creates from itself and sends to the pack to guide them. Use the normal Totem background rules and the appropriate spirit totem description to create the spirit.<br /> <br /> 3) Pack totems, like all spirits, reside in a particular space and time. It is not a diembodied presence somehow &quot;with&quot; one or more of the PCs. It has a specific ephemeral body that resides in the Penumbra. In terms of what it can do, use the Powers the PCs selected for the Totem using the Totem background. My recommendation is to treat it like an NPC, and to ask your players for guidance for all the questions you have. Who is able to speak to the pack totem, where does it reside when the PCs aren't interacting with it, what are its preferences to be located when it is not actively helping the PCs, how do the PCs make sure the totem know where they are and can go to them when needed, etc. These are things that are very often to overlook - in my experience most STs and PCs simply assume the pack totem is somehow always present, but that is not so. This should be a conversation between players and STs so that they know how you will be running it, and you can adjust your roleplay of this NPC to meet their expectations.<br /> <br /> 4) The creation/presence of a caern typically attracts many other Gaian spirits to the caern besides the actual caern totem. Many of these spirits will choose to ally themselves with the caern totem for protection, similar to a brood. They will act as the caern totem's eyes and ears outside the bawn, and likely defenders within the bawn. I recommend writing up a small list of such spirits so you can run them as NPCs if needed. Just make them appropriate for the territory around the caern. In a caern set around a music recording company in the city, you don't want wolf, bison, and owl spirits. But there's probably spirits of Rats, Cockroaches, Pigeons, maybe some domesticated animals, and the like, perhaps with some Squirrels, other bird spirits, and similar park animals if there is some park nearby with trees and such. There may also be some friendly Electricity Elementals, Water Elementals (from the water pipes), and Weaver oriented members of Cockroach's brood.<br /> <br /> Then you have Rites like the Rite of the Shrouded Glen that has likely already been performed in order to protect and hide the caern. There are also a variety of fetishes that can be used to help warn the caern's Garou if there is some trouble in the local area.<br /> <br /> In terms of Garou actively protecting the caern (and its Penumbra), any sept will likely make arrangements to make sure the caern is adequately defended. The Master of the Rite is probably always at the caern and likely has the strongest relationship with the caern totem, and should be the Garou to best understand the needs of the caern totem in the Umbra. At the same time, it is conceivable some other Garou like the Gatekeeper, Keeper of the Land, or Sept Leader might fill that roll. In terms of actually protecting the caern, that is the duty of the Caern Warder and any Gaou he selects as guardians though I suspect he would listen heavily to whatever Garou is closest to the caern totem in regards to ensuring there are sufficient Garou present or able to quickly enter the Penumbra.<br /> <br /> In a small level one caern, there likely is not many Garou available who can dedicate themselves to full time defense of the caern/Penmubra outside the leadership. But for ease of play, I'd likely have 3 Garou at any one time be present - one in the real world, one in the Penumbra, and a third resting who can relieve one of the Garou as needed. Perhaps this group of three being switched out for another group of three or pack every so often to make sure the burden is equitably spread and those Garou on watch as Guardians who complete their other goals.<br /> <br /> A level one caern in particular isn't very noticeable or attracts attention and needs less active defensive measures than stronger types. Hiding is probably a better strategy than obvious defenses. Even in a city, there are not spirits everywhere. Weaver spirits will be concentrated in the centers of town of strong Weaver activity where there are Weaver domains, and the Banes will be concentrated in areas where there is high Wyrm taint and acitivty with Wyrm Tunnel domains. Those spirits will occasionally explore, or be on a mission that takes them elsewhere, but likely the caern is located in a place far from those spots. As long as the local Garou make sure their immediate vicinity does not naturally attract their attention or allow the conglomeration of Triatic energies, the rank might escape much notice.<br /> <br /> 5) My recommendations would be Axis Mundi and the very first Umbra: the Velvet Shadow (I prefer it to the Revised version, it provides better advice to STs and has some information not carried over into later versions of the Umbra soucebooks). For an urban centered chronicle, you may find the Book of the City and Book of the Weaver helpful. <br /> <br /> \u200bI also like the first edition Storyteller's Handbook for similar reasons I prefer the first edition Umbra. It does not have so many things about spirits, but it does have some good advice on city chronicles and a good section on antagonists.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-11-08T12:55:07-08:00","id":1496529,"title":"","content":"As usual a very nice and detailed response from <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3046_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3046\" data-vbnamecard=\"3046\">Black Fox</a> . Also cool to see the old ST's Handbook called out, as I've really gotten a lot of mileage out of that. I'm curious about what made you like the original Umbra more than Revised?<br /> <br /> I'd also like to mention that the availability of the pack totem to the group is directly tied to the expenditure of points on the totem itself. So while by default it's not available, there is the perk for two points &quot;The totem is nearly always with the pack&quot;. That's the one that causes the perception of availability and that the totem doesn't have other things to do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-11-08T22:19:22-08:00","id":1496575,"title":"","content":"\u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1496498-new-st-question-spirit-confusion?p=1496529#post1496529\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Also cool to see the old ST's Handbook called out, as I've really gotten a lot of mileage out of that.</div> </div> </div> </div>In my opinion, it's a neglected, hidden gem of a sourcebook. It has a lot of cool stuff that is not mentioned again in subsequent sourcebooks.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1496498-new-st-question-spirit-confusion?p=1496529#post1496529\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I'm curious about what made you like the original Umbra more than Revised?</div> </div> </div> </div>There's several reasons why I like the original Umbra sourcebook. First, it gives us maps, no matter how crudely, of what each of the Near Realms are supposed to be like. The visualization helps me a lot in terms of how I, as the ST, should describe things if the PCs attempt to navigate it. Second, I think its description of Umbral travel and hazards are more helpful than Revised. Third, it has a better selection of spirits, some of whom are never mentioned again - Revised mainly grabs spirits already published elsewhere like in Axis Mundi, but with less variety. Fourth, I think its description of the Dream Zone and dreams is much superior to Revised. Fifth, there's a lot of hidden trivia sprinkled throughout it that is never mentioned again. I find such trivia to be very illuminating as to how the original creators envisioned the game to be.<br /> <br /> In general, I like a lot of WoD 1e material over its Revised counterparts. It could often be too gonzo, too rough around the edges, weaker mechanics, and have a lot of gaps, but it is brimming with ideas and atmosphere. While Revised did a good job of trying to bring a lot of concepts together in a more professional matter, I find it often forgets a lot of information and flavor I find essential in its attempts to synthesize and combine material from multiple groups. Revised material loses a lot of the &quot;magic&quot; and appeal to me. While I originally preferred a lot of the Revised material, over time it's the 1e or 2e material I keep going back to for inspiration or clues in how to run the game. (I don't always prefer 1e material. I think the 2e Players Guide is the best version.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1496498-new-st-question-spirit-confusion?p=1496529#post1496529\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd also like to mention that the availability of the pack totem to the group is directly tied to the expenditure of points on the totem itself. So while by default it's not available, there is the perk for two points &quot;The totem is nearly always with the pack&quot;. That's the one that causes the perception of availability and that the totem doesn't have other things to do.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah. I still think that needs to be depicted accurately in game. Meaning it is still only one ephemeral body and can only be at one place at any one time. It's not some disembodied head that appears over each of the PCs. I interpret that to mean the spirit tries on its own to always be relatively near the pack as opposed to possibly wandering off. If the pack splits up, it prefers to stick with the part that has the most members, with preference given to the location of the Garou that &quot;controls&quot; the individual power benefit of the pack totem. But different people may interpret that &quot;power&quot; differently.<br /> <br /> In my experience, the roleplaying opportunities of the pack totem is often downplayed or ignored. Many STs and players don't really treat it like an NPC, just a collection of benefits. I try very hard to encourage players to collaboratively build their pack totem - the name it is known by, its physical appearance, its personality and quirks, how it approaches its role as &quot;father&quot; to the pack, and so on. Which PC does it like the most and which the least? What areas of the Penumbra does it prefer to dwell? What are its opinions on other Garou, spirits, and so on?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-11-09T04:11:16-08:00","id":1496584,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1496498-new-st-question-spirit-confusion?p=1496575#post1496575\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200bYeah. I still think that needs to be depicted accurately in game. Meaning it is still only one ephemeral body and can only be at one place at any one time. It's not some disembodied head that appears over each of the PCs. I interpret that to mean the spirit tries on its own to always be relatively near the pack as opposed to possibly wandering off. If the pack splits up, it prefers to stick with the part that has the most members, with preference given to the location of the Garou that &quot;controls&quot; the individual power benefit of the pack totem. But different people may interpret that &quot;power&quot; differently.<br /> <br /> In my experience, the roleplaying opportunities of the pack totem is often downplayed or ignored. Many STs and players don't really treat it like an NPC, just a collection of benefits. I try very hard to encourage players to collaboratively build their pack totem - the name it is known by, its physical appearance, its personality and quirks, how it approaches its role as &quot;father&quot; to the pack, and so on. Which PC does it like the most and which the least? What areas of the Penumbra does it prefer to dwell? What are its opinions on other Garou, spirits, and so on?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, you've talked me into grabbing the original Umbra PDF. I'm a strong believer that Revised was the golden age for Werewolf, but cool and weird exotica about the spiritual worlds and interacting with them sounds cool. Also really curious what the maps look like, though i believe they may be a bit of a dissapointment.<br /> <br /> As for the totem, not treating it like a full NPC is a real waste. It's a character of its own, with its own agenda, duties and bosses to report to. It does care for the pack and needs the pack to become more powerful and perform its duty, but it's an extra NPC that needs to be developed and embraced fully. The fact that it can communicate with characters and create positive and negative relationships and experiences is just a cherry on top. It's also a fun way to interact with the cast as the ST.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-11-09T07:31:52-08:00","id":1496593,"title":"","content":"For the Music Spirit, it might be useful to look at broader musical concepts that are easy to transfer into a code of behavior or paradigm. Unison, harmony, resonance for a more Weavery type or contrapoint, enharmony, dissonance for a more Wyld type for example.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-11-09T09:27:48-08:00","id":1496601,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1496498-new-st-question-spirit-confusion?p=1496584#post1496584\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, you've talked me into grabbing the original Umbra PDF. I'm a strong believer that Revised was the golden age for Werewolf, but cool and weird exotica about the spiritual worlds and interacting with them sounds cool. Also really curious what the maps look like, though i believe they may be a bit of a dissapointment.</div> </div> </div> </div>As I said the maps are very crude, but for certain realms, like the Cyberrealm, the map's visual provided me a very different idea of the realm's layout than just having the text description in revised. And the fact that there even are maps for places like the Legendary Realm, the Summer Country, and Pangea was surprising. Simply having those would help me construct a scenario or adventure if a group of PCs quested there and I wanted them to traverse through it for some time. Otherwise I'd just have some very generic terrain in mind. For me, having some guidance like this spurs my creativity more. Some realms do not have maps (Battelground, Scar, and Wolfhome among them).<br /> <br /> Of course 80-90% of the content is practically the same. I found that 10-20% difference very helpful to me, but your mileage may vary a lot. I didn't mind picking up the PDF, the money was worth it to me. And if someone asked me which version should they pick up if they wanted only one, I would recommend the first. But if you really liked the Revised version and already have it, it's hard for me to tell how much picking up the 1e as well would add to your game. Hope it's a good purchase for you.<br /> <br /> But I do think there are some good bits in there that I don't remember from the other versions some of which do clarify certain concepts, and there are stronger emphasis on certain sections of the Umbra, and overall I think the book does a better job of trying to be a tool to help the ST run the game in the Umbra rather than just a book of descriptions. And of course it has the original perspective of 1e of trying to prepare a comprehensive setting for all the WoD games (Arcadia Gateway is home to fairies, heavily influenced by the Ars Magica setting at this point since Changeling has not only not been written but still advertised as Faerie) as opposed to the Revised perspective that each game is separate from the others (and thus there are no actual fairies in the Arcadia Gateway just &quot;spirits&quot;).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-11-10T07:20:26-08:00","id":1496692,"title":"","content":"Firstly, I just want to say thanks again to everyone who provided a response and guidance to my questions. Transitioning from a more rigid numbers heavy TTRPG like Pathfinder to WOD has been tough, especially so since we are all pretty new to the settings and themes. I really want to have a firm understanding on how to portray the Penumbra to my players, because they all seem to be pretty confused by what its like as well. The information that you all have provided as been helpful!<br /> <br /> 1. I have decided that the Caern will have inhabited by a Spirit of Harmony. Applying the information as I understand it, this spirit would be a unique Jaggling. Do they have names? I have a visual that I like, and a ban. I am going to have the spirit require that all rites/communication associated with it be recited in chord. It will provide bonuses to pack tactics or other actions taking in tandem with others. <br /> <br /> 2. For the Pack totem, can someone be very specific and just give me a concrete example of how they work? I understand the mechanical benefits of how the totems work. The players put their points into a pool and bought City Father. So City Father gives them some sort of mystical blessing in the form of the mechanical stat bonuses they get. If they step into the Penumbra, City Father is not just sitting there watching over them. He is off doing his own thing. And by City Father, its actually just an Avatar Jaggling of City Father which is like a part of him? What book is the concept of Avatar Jagglings described? Does the pack totem have different names? I'm really just trying to understand what this looks like. If you are at a Sept moot and peek into the Penumbra, is there a bunch of pack totems sitting around? Is it a common occurrence to be fighting alongside your pack totem when in the Penumbra?<br /> <br /> Thanks!<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27612-awesomeotter","name":"Awesomeotter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27612_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-11-10T09:13:42-08:00","id":1496701,"title":"","content":"This is what I do:<br /> <br /> 1. I always give Jaggling spirits names that they are known by the Garou and other spirits. That is not their True Name which is kept secret. It is just a name others refer to them, and the spirit might be known by multiple names. Also remember there are names that are pronounceable by Garou/humans, and what their Spirit Speech name might be. For example, a Wind spirit might be called by a name like &quot;Summer Breeze&quot; by Garou, but it's Spirit Speech name would be more like a pleasant breeze hitting your face, and the Theurge who knows Spirit Speech simply translated it as &quot;Summer Breeze&quot; to his pack/sept. Neither would be the spirit's True Name.<br /> <br /> Because Gafflings are supposed to be less intelligent, any known names they have are given to them by Garou in similar ways a human gives their pets names. The Gafflings will quickly realize that such names are in reference to them and will respond to them. (They have True Names as well.) In contrast, Jagglings tend to have their own names independently because they are intelligent creatures with wills of their own. Though they often have other names given to them by Garou, other Fera, occultists, and so on. That is how I run it in my own chronicles.<br /> <br /> 2) The pack totem may or may not be nearby the pack. It really depends on what the players bought for their Totem points, and how they interact with the totem. PCs who are more engaged with their totem, seek its advice, tend to its needs, perform favors for it, etc. should have a much more responsive totem than PCs who mostly ignore the totem until they want something. And of course, the pack can always request the totem try to be close by and so on. Just treat the spirit like you would any other person. The pack totem wants the pack to succeed and guide it, it is their &quot;parent&quot; after all. But children often neglect their parent. The parent may still love them, but the relationship will be more distant. In my experience, the PCs typically want a strong relationship with their totem, but inadvertently neglect it because so much happens in the material world. I would work OOC with the players to help them match their expectations with their actual behavior so you are not punishing them for something they don't mean to be doing.<br /> <br /> And don't forget that not all areas of the Penumbra are the same. If the PCs are in place in the material world whose corresponding Penumbra has a Weaver domain and lots of Pattern Spiders and such, even a City Father spirit will likely stay far enough away to be safe. Though in other areas of the city Penumbra, a City Father pack totem might always be very close. On the other hand, if the pack leaves the city and travels through the rural areas, wilderness, or even to another city, the City Father pack totem might just remain in its city. Likewise a wilderness Garou pack under Stag entering a city might have their stag pack totem stay behind. Though a Garou pack under Rat, Squirrel, or Raccoon might have their pack totem come into the city - just like those animals are often found in urban environments. Play the pack totem in a way that makes sense to you as the ST.<br /> <br /> If you have W20, on page 327 under the Incarna description it states &quot;Pack totems are the most commonly encountered form of 'incarnate&quot; avatar; however, as spiritual servants, pack totems demonstrate power on par with Jagglings.&quot; There are various other mentions of jaggling avatars scattered through other books. You'll need to find them yourself. I recommend Axis Mundi as the best source for a lot of spirit questions.<br /> <br /> Typically the players will want to name the pack totem OCC in my experience. IC that might be something the PCs called the totem after meeting him, or it could be the totem providing that name to them. If they don't and just want to call it Stag or Rat or Pittsburgh, that's fine too. I always create names for the pack totems of any NPC packs, and in the one case where my players did not originally create their own name for their totem that was enough to inspire them to do so once they realized the NPCs have names for their own pack totems. It's more of a flavor text thing. And of course this name is not the spirit's &quot;True Name&quot; although the spirit might be willing to trust giving a character his true name if their relationship and trust becomes very, very close over a long time.<br /> <br /> Typically during moots I do have the pack totems present in the Penumbra. If they are not, there is generally an explanation. It could be something as mundane as the pack totem is performing some critical mission while the moot is going on. Or it might be because of spirit politics. If the caern spirit is Pegasus, and a pack has Fenris as a totem, the pack totem may want to stay away because those two spirits dislike each other and it would be worse if it showed up. Or for a pack under Rat at a caern whose totem is an Owl, that Rat spirit might be afraid Owl will eat him and therefore stay away or hide or act in some other appropriate way.<br /> <br /> I usually find that PCs want/expect their pack totem to be with them when they enter the Penumbra and to accompany them. Whether they fight in combat or not really depends on how the players built the totem (Rage, Charms, Essence, etc.) and their expectations for it. Usually PCs under a War totem build the totem spirit so it can fight well. They might not do that if it is some other kind of totem. Depending on they built it, the PCs may or may not want it to fight in combat. If I as a PC built an Owl spirit with low Rage and high Gnosis, and did not give it many combat powers, I would not want to risk that pack totem by having it be in combat. On the other hand, if for some strange reason I built a Fenris spirit to also not be a combat spirit, the spirit's very nature should compel it to want to fight anyway. Or sometimes the spirit might be useful in some combats, but not in others. Remember that spirits driven to zero Essence discorporate and go into Slumber. Packs will want to prevent that from happening. Use common sense and help the players out when they are designing the totem so it's appropriate for what they want it to do.<br /> <br /> <br /> What I do isn't law. Work out what is best for your ST style and what is fun for your players. Worry less about whether it is &quot;right&quot;, and more for what works best for your gaming table.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}